----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, N: b1-a3, N: b1-c3, P: b2-b4, P: b2-b3, P: c2-c4, P: c2-c3, P: d2-d4, P: d2-d3, P: e2-e4, P: e2-e3, P: f2-f4, P: f2-f3, N: g1-f3, N: g1-h3, P: g2-g4, P: g2-g3, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Evaluation: As White, I have a full set of undeveloped pieces and the first-move advantage. The priority in the opening is to control the center, develop minor pieces (knights and bishops), and prepare for king safety (castling). Several pawn moves can help control the center (e2-e4, d2-d4), while knight moves like N: g1-f3 or N: b1-c3 are strong developing moves that also support central control.\n\nKey considerations:\n- P: e2-e4 immediately stakes claim on central squares (d5, f5) and opens diagonals for the queen and bishop.\n- N: g1-f3 develops a knight with tempo potential (attacking e5 if Black plays ...e5) and avoids blocking the e-pawn.\n- P: d2-d4 similarly fights for the center and opens lines for the queen and light-squared bishop.\n- Moving pawns two squares (e.g., e2-e4 instead of e2-e3) gains more space early.\n\nAmong these, e2-e4 is historically one of the most common and effective opening moves, promoting rapid development and central dominance.\n\nReason: Playing P: e2-e4 maximizes central control, frees the queen and bishop for active play, and aligns with classical opening principles. It’s a cornerstone of many high-level openings (e.g., Italian Game, Ruy López, Open Games).\n\nAnswer: P: e2-e4'}
