 purpose now.\n- **B: c1-d2** is passive but enables castling if needed.\n\nAmong these, **N: b1-c3** stands out as a principled developing move, supporting central control, preparing for kingside castling, and not blocking any pawns. It also defends the e4 square indirectly if Black tries to expand.\n\nReason:  \nDeveloping the knight to c3 is a cornerstone of many openings (e.g., Italian, Queen’s Gambit). It increases control over the center (d5, e4), prepares for harmonious development, and keeps options open for both king-side castling and central play. It does not prematurely commit the queen or weaken the pawn structure.\n\nAnswer: N: b1-c3"}
